    "shop_smart":  [
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eShelf-stable gnocchi is the cheap kind.\u003c/strong\u003e It lives in the pasta aisle, not the refrigerated case, it keeps for months, and it cooks in about three minutes right in the broth.",
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eBuy a frozen turkey breast roast and portion it.\u003c/strong\u003e Turkey breast is at its cheapest around the holidays. Cook one, pull it, and freeze it in bags for soups like this one.",
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eHeavy cream and milk together, not cream alone.\u003c/strong\u003e Cutting the cream with milk keeps the soup silky, drops the cost, and drops the fat without anyone noticing.",
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eFrozen chopped spinach goes in straight from the box.\u003c/strong\u003e It costs less than fresh, it does not wilt into nothing, and there is no washing involved."
                   ],
    "make_it":  [
                    "Weigh your empty mixing pot and write the number down for portioning later.",
                    "Dice the onion, celery and carrots small and sweat them in the olive oil in your big pot over medium heat until soft, about 8 minutes.",
                    "Add the minced garlic, the dried thyme and the Italian seasoning and stir for a minute until fragrant.",
                    "Pour in the chicken broth, bring it to a simmer, and let the vegetables get fully tender.",
                    "Add the gnocchi and cook until they float and turn soft, only a few minutes. Do not walk away here.",
                    "Stir in the cooked turkey breast, the thawed chopped spinach, the heavy cream and the milk, then bring it back to a bare simmer without letting it boil.",
                    "Season with salt and black pepper and let it sit off the heat for a few minutes so it thickens up."
                ],
